Subject: gl: serve the anchor's capability limits the filter and the table dropped

A live detector read `gpu.gl-caps-contradict-backend` off the shipped
linux-x64 artifact: renderer "ANGLE (Intel, Intel(R) UHD Graphics 630
(0x00009BC5) Direct3D11 vs_5_0 ps_5_0, D3D11)" beside a 16384x16384 viewport,
where Direct3D shader model 5 mandates 32767. Thirteen of the anchor's
measured parameters were being served from the host. This patch is the serving
half of the fix; the other half is in scripts/generate-dispersion-tables.py,
which never emitted five of them, and this patch is inert without it.

One wrong belief, written down in four places, and only one of them was the
bug anyone had noticed: that a GL capability value is always a positive
scalar. The generator's anchor_gl_layer filter encoded it by accepting only
MAX_-prefixed integers >= 1. The serving table encoded it by having no
direction for a floor. And config/profile.schema.json encoded it a third time
as `minimum: 1` on gl_limits' additionalProperties -- which would have
rejected the correct data outright, since MAX_SERVER_WAIT_TIMEOUT is measured
0 and MIN_PROGRAM_TEXEL_OFFSET is measured -8. That schema is a DATA_ASSET
copied by scripts/sync-packages.py into python/apostate/assets and
npm/assets, so the false assumption shipped in the packages too. And the
loader in base/apostate/profile.cc encoded it a fourth time, refusing any
gl_limits entry that was not > 0 and logging an ERROR line per launch for
each -- so even a profile that carried MIN_PROGRAM_TEXEL_OFFSET and
MAX_SERVER_WAIT_TIMEOUT correctly arrived at the serving hooks without them.
Fixing the generator alone, or the serving table alone, would have changed
nothing for those two.

Recorded because the first attempt at that schema fix was wrong in an
instructive way. It named the two exceptions as explicit properties to carry
their bounds, and scripts/check-schema-wiring.py rejected it: gl_limits is
consumed by a wholesale loop in base/apostate/profile.cc, and the checker
requires every explicitly-named child to appear as a string literal in the
consuming file. Those literals live in ui/gl, not profile.cc, so the named
shape asserted a wiring that does not exist. That was a true positive, the
same class as a falsely-enforced ledger edge, and the right fix was to drop
the bound from additionalProperties and state the reason in the description.

Three separate defects produced one symptom, and they are worth separating
because only the third is a policy question.

First, six parameters were in the compiled table and served from the host
anyway, because nothing in kApostateGlLimits named them:
UNIFORM_BUFFER_OFFSET_ALIGNMENT, MAX_PROGRAM_TEXEL_OFFSET,
MIN_PROGRAM_TEXEL_OFFSET, MAX_SERVER_WAIT_TIMEOUT and the two aliased ranges.
This is the same gap MAX_TEXTURE_LOD_BIAS had before patch 0036 and was found
the same way -- by diffing an anchor against what the binary actually served,
rather than by reading the table and believing it. UNIFORM_BUFFER_OFFSET_
ALIGNMENT is the sharpest: D3D11 mandates 256, the anchors measure 256, a
software rasteriser reports 16, and a page reads it in one call.

Second, MIN_PROGRAM_TEXEL_OFFSET is a floor and the clamp assumed every limit
is a ceiling. Claiming -8 where the driver allows -32 is the *narrower* claim,
so std::min served the driver's -32 and discarded the anchor's number. The
direction is now a property of the entry. The same distinction applies to the
two-element limits: an aliased range is an interval, so intersecting it with
the driver takes the higher floor and the lower ceiling, while
MAX_VIEWPORT_DIMS is a width and a height -- two independent ceilings, where
taking the higher of two widths would over-claim.

Third, the enforcement test. 0103 decided, correctly, to serve the claim where
the backend enforces nothing and keep min(claim, driver) where it does. The
criterion it stated is "does this backend enforce what it reports". The test it
shipped asks something narrower -- whether the ANGLE implementation is
kSwiftShader -- and that is an under-approximation of 0103's own principle
rather than a deliberate narrowing of it. ANGLE over Mesa llvmpipe reports
kOpenGL and is indistinguishable by enum from ANGLE over a real GL driver.

That is not hypothetical, and it is the configuration the detector read. On the
Linux deployment target, headed under Xvfb, the composed identity stops
Chromium's blocklist matching llvmpipe -- patch 0007 feeds the profile's driver
strings to the blocklist deliberately, and patch 0108 documents that a
blocklist which does see llvmpipe disables WebGL outright. So ANGLE/OpenGL on
llvmpipe is what runs, BackendEnforcesGlLimits() returned true, and min()
clamped every served limit back to a software rasteriser's table. Measured on
the same box with the same seed, forcing the backend and changing nothing else:

  default                  viewport 16384, UBO align 16, MAX_SAMPLES 4
  --use-angle=swiftshader  viewport 32767, UBO align 256, MAX_SAMPLES 16

MAX_SAMPLES 16 against a host reporting 4 is the discriminator: the claim is
served, so 0103 works when it fires. It was not firing.

llvmpipe was measured against 0103's criterion with 0103's own method, on the
shipped 152.0.7977.83 linux-x64 artifact: texStorage2D at 1x and 2x the
reported MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE, texStorage3D at 8x MAX_3D_TEXTURE_SIZE and
renderbufferStorageMultisample at 4x MAX_SAMPLES all return glError 0 with an
incomplete framebuffer. It enforces nothing, identically to the SwiftShader
control run beside it. So this is a correction to the test, not a widening of
the policy: the set of backends 0103 intended to cover is unchanged, and one
that always belonged in it is now in it.

Still not IsSoftwareGLImplementation(), for 0103's reason unchanged: that also
covers D3D11 WARP, whose enforcement has not been measured, and guessing
permissively is how an over-claim becomes a failing draw call. WARP cannot
arise on the Linux and macOS targets, and llvmpipe is Linux-only -- on
macOS/Metal the clamp stays and stays correct.

Why MAX_VIEWPORT_DIMS is served here and not through ANGLE, because the
obvious fix does not work and the next reader will try it. Patch 0039 already
consumes MAX_VIEWPORT_DIMS_WIDTH and MAX_VIEWPORT_DIMS_HEIGHT in
ApplyProfileIntegerCaps, so emitting those two keys looks sufficient. It is
not: that function only ever does min(caps, claim). It can lower ANGLE's
number and never raise it, so on any host whose driver reports 16384 --
llvmpipe and Metal both -- it clamps the anchor's 32767 straight back to 16384
and changes nothing. The enforcement test is what makes the claim servable and
it lives on this path, so the range is served here. The 0039 consumer is left
alone; it still narrows correctly when the claim is the smaller number.

Last, the silent case is now loud, in both places it was quiet.

In the GL hooks: a composed profile that carries a GPU identity and an empty
gl_limits is precisely the defect that shipped, and every hook returned early
on it, so the host's numbers went to the page under a claimed device's
renderer string and looked like success. That pairing now logs once, naming
the claimed renderer.

In --fingerprint-explain: the cross-backend limitation said "the identity and
the capability limits are served as composed". On the shipped artifact the
second half was false, so the one diagnostic an operator runs to find out what
is honoured was actively reassuring them about the broken thing. The sentence
no longer asserts anything about limits. Instead compose.cc counts gl_limits
in the profile it actually merged and reports that, and reports the zero case
as its own limitation. A count taken from the merged profile cannot drift away
from the truth the way a hardcoded sentence did, because there is nothing left
to keep in sync.

Nothing here is page-visible except the served numbers, and no switch is
added. On an enforcing backend the behaviour is unchanged: min() for ceilings,
max() for the one floor, warning included.

Ledger: webgl.parameters, webgl2.parameters, gpu.angle-backend, headless.gpu-stack.
